# Local database connection. Copy this file to packages/db/.env — it is the one
# place a local connection string lives, and apps/api reads it from here too, so
# it is never duplicated per app.
#
# This file is for LOCAL development only. Production is a separate file,
# .env.neon (see .env.neon.example), read by nothing except `pnpm db:prod:*`.
# Keeping them apart is what stops `pnpm db:reset` from ever meaning "wipe
# production" — the destructive scripts refuse to run unless the string below
# points at localhost.
#
# In production nothing reads a .env file at all: Vercel supplies DATABASE_URL
# as a project environment variable, on the api project only. The web project
# never connects to the database.

# The Docker container from compose.yaml, started by `pnpm db:up`. Credentials
# are deliberately boring and deliberately committed: nothing sensitive reaches
# this database and the port is bound to the loopback interface.
#
# One variable is enough locally. Production needs two because Neon's pooled
# "-pooler" host is what the serverless function connects through while
# migrations must avoid it; a local Postgres has no pooler, so this single
# string plays both parts via prisma.config.ts's fallback from DIRECT_URL to
# DATABASE_URL.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/moodnight"

# Optional. `prisma migrate dev` needs a throwaway database to detect drift and
# creates one automatically when the role may create databases — which the local
# superuser may. Set this only if that fails: point it at a scratch database
# that migrate may wipe at will.
# SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=

# No Docker? `pnpm db exec prisma dev` starts a Postgres that the Prisma CLI
# ships with — no container, no account — and prints a DATABASE_URL and
# SHADOW_DATABASE_URL to paste here. Nothing else in the workflow changes.
#
# A Neon branch works as a local database too, and is the way to develop
# without Docker on a machine that cannot run it. Use a branch of your own, not
# the one the deployed api reads: the guard on the destructive scripts only
# knows whether a host is local, so a remote development database trades that
# protection for the convenience, and `pnpm db:reset` becomes yours to aim
# carefully. Point DATABASE_URL at the branch's unpooled host and expect the
# destructive scripts to refuse until you say otherwise.
